name air-awacs-blind
timeout 300

# THE MOST DANGEROUS LINE IN doc/modules/air/module.md, MEASURED: an early-warning aircraft MOVES AN
# ANTENNA. IT NEVER CREATES A TRACK.
#
# THE CAST
#   magic   friendly `e3`, a MOVER row on a racetrack at 9 000 m. Its APY-1 sees 400 km and its rotodome
#           turns once per 10.0 s [DERIVED from the sourced 6 rpm]. What it publishes is an FBNetReport:
#           a POINT rebuilt from its OWN anonymous echo, carrying its own look age, with NO id field and
#           NO team field. It cannot say hostile (no such value exists in the tree), it cannot name a
#           unit, and it cannot hand anybody a track.
#   fox     friendly `mig21`, the member. Its RP-22 sees 20 km through a +-30 x +-10 deg field — a
#           QUARTER of the sky an F-16's CRM covers — and it cannot look down at all. What the cue buys
#           it is TWO command-bus entries: an antenna azimuth and an antenna elevation, each latency-
#           charged and each rejectable. It must then FIND, FIRM over its own looks and GATE the target
#           with its own set.
#   raider  hostile `tu95`, a MOVER on a straight leg. It radiates nothing and shoots nothing.
#
# WHAT TO READ
#   events.log       `net CUE azDeg=... elDeg=... rangeM=... ageS=...` on fox, then — and only then —
#                    `radar RADAR_CONTACT unit=fox`. The cue's `ageS` is the SUM of the two staleness
#                    terms: the node's own look age at the moment it reported (up to 10.0 s, its own
#                    frame) plus the age the receiving terminal computed. At a 250 m/s target that is
#                    >= 2.5 km of positional error fox has to search out itself, WHICH IS WHY A 400 km
#                    SENSOR IS NEVER A FIRING SOLUTION — and it is arithmetic, not a rule.
#   telemetry_fox.csv  air_cue_age_s (the two terms summed, -1 when uncued), air_cues, radar_*
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 (TIMEOUT) is the expected verdict — nobody in this file carries a weapon and
# nothing is meant to die. What the run MEASURES is the pair (`net CUE` count > 0, `radar CONTACT` on
# fox) and the SEQUENCE between them. The companion file air-awacs-blind.fbm flies the identical
# geometry with fox's own radar failed: there the cue lines are unchanged and the contact lines are
# ZERO, which is the whole boundary in one diff.

unit magic
  team friendly
  module e3
  spawn 46.60000 6.40000 9000 90.0 240
  set orbit 60000 900
  set drag_threat_s 0                 # ordered to hold the orbit: the honest way to say "never runs"
  wp 46.60000 7.20000 9000 240

unit fox
  team friendly
  module mig21
  spawn 46.90000 6.75000 6000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set task intercept
  set fcr off
  set store 1 r60
  set store 2 r60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  wp 46.90000 7.60000 6000 450

unit raider
  team hostile
  module tu95
  spawn 46.90000 7.55000 6000 270.0 380
  wp 46.90000 6.10000 6000 380
